\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10}{\ignorespaces Literature context: when thinking mode helps vs.\ hurts safety. The matrix shows that the effect of thinking mode depends on attack type. Reasoning-targeted attacks (H-CoT) exploit visible reasoning chains, causing safety to degrade. General multi-turn attacks (this work, baseline) show that thinking mode provides protection---likely because extended reasoning enables identification of adversarial framing. Structured reasoning defenses (ARMOR) achieve strong protection. The finding that TEMPEST attacks are partially mitigated by thinking mode suggests that general multi-turn attacks do not specifically exploit reasoning chains, unlike H-CoT.}}{21}{figure.caption.15}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:literature}{{10}{21}{Literature context: when thinking mode helps vs.\ hurts safety. The matrix shows that the effect of thinking mode depends on attack type. Reasoning-targeted attacks (H-CoT) exploit visible reasoning chains, causing safety to degrade. General multi-turn attacks (this work, baseline) show that thinking mode provides protection---likely because extended reasoning enables identification of adversarial framing. Structured reasoning defenses (ARMOR) achieve strong protection. The finding that TEMPEST attacks are partially mitigated by thinking mode suggests that general multi-turn attacks do not specifically exploit reasoning chains, unlike H-CoT}{figure.caption.15}{}}
